## One platform, many divisions

Kita serves divisions that work very differently. Operations runs inventory across five gas products. Logistics plans and logs deliveries. Sales and marketing track customers and pricing, finance watches costs, and legal manages contracts and licences. Two things hold it together: one design system, and data that moves between modules instead of being typed in twice.

{% Modules items=[{name: "Ton-kilometres", description: "Trips, distances and freight performance against target"}, {name: "Dashboards", description: "One the CEO builds himself, and a matrix for comparing periods"}, {name: "Reports", description: "Sales and customer performance, transit loss, pricing, product sourcing"}, {name: "Inventory", description: "Operations’ stock log and management"}, {name: "Contracts", description: "Agreements, obligations, payments and reminders"}, {name: "Licences & permits", description: "Regulatory paperwork the business must keep current"}, {name: "Assets", description: "Products, and the individual units of each"}] /%}

Alongside those: fleet tracking, fuel requests, issues, a stack list, depot insights and an exchange-rate tool.

## Ton-kilometres: the module used every day

A tonne-kilometre is one tonne of cargo moved one kilometre. It’s how Asiko measures freight work: what deliveries cost, how its own fleet compares with third-party trucks, and how each truck performs against its target.

Before Kita, logistics officers worked it out by hand in spreadsheets, maintained the formulas, and rebuilt the charts for every report: by location, by business channel, by truck category, against monthly targets. Every report was out of date by the time it was finished.

I split the module into an input and an output. **Trips** is where an officer logs each delivery: the truck, its location, the payload pattern (loaded out and empty back, empty out and loaded back, or empty both ways), the cargo and the route. Kita subtracts the truck’s tare weight, recorded once in Assets, so every tonne-kilometre counts cargo alone, and it works out the distance itself. The **performance report** turns those trips into the figures Logistics and Finance plan with: trips, tonnes moved, tonne-kilometres, the average per trip, own fleet against third-party, and budget against actual.

I kept Trips close to the spreadsheet officers already knew: a dense table with filters, because that’s how they think about the work.

{% Figure src="/media/kita/trips.webp" label="Trips: the table officers log deliveries in" caption="Close to the spreadsheet officers already knew: one row per trip, with the cargo, payload pattern and tonne-kilometres worked out." /%}

