## A dashboard the CEO builds himself

Kita has a lot of reports, and the CEO wanted oversight across many of them at a glance. But what he needed to see changed from day to day and week to week, so another fixed overview screen would have been out of date as soon as it shipped.

Instead of designing one, I turned the charts, metrics and stats from across Kita into widgets, and gave him a dashboard he can add to, rearrange and edit himself. There are more than fifteen widgets now, and he uses it.

{% Figure src="/media/kita/dashboard.webp" label="The CEO’s dashboard, built from widgets" caption="Widgets drawn from reports across Kita, which the CEO adds, arranges and edits himself." /%}

## Comparing like with like

The CEO’s quarterly reports to the board need context. In this business, performance is judged against similar periods under similar conditions, so his idea was to compare them side by side: the current period against the prior one, and actual against budget, in one view. The charts didn’t share a layout, so no single comparison pattern fitted them all.

## Contracts, and the 53 laptops

Contracts started as a request for a simple tracker. Talking to legal, procurement and operations showed the real problem was obligations: renewal dates, payments and deadlines that nobody was reminded about. So I designed contract management instead: a directory, a detail view with activity, revisions, payments and documents, reusable contract types, one shared list of counterparties, and reminders. Licences and permits followed.

{% Figure src="/media/kita/contract.webp" label="A contract’s detail page" caption="Where a contract stands, what’s been paid and what’s due next, on one page." /%}

In Assets, staff were registering 53 identical laptops one at a time. Separating products (the make and model) from units (each physical laptop) fixed that, and shaped every view after it.

{% Figure src="/media/kita/products.webp" label="Assets: products, each with its own units" caption="A product is registered once; each physical unit hangs off it." /%}

## What hasn’t landed

Assets and Contracts are live but lightly used. Assets, because the company is rethinking how it creates and tracks assets as its business model changes. Contracts, because most of the legal team has turned over and the new team is still settling in. Shipping a module isn’t the same as people using it.
